        exp: "Rule: Under UCC § 2-209(1), an agreement modifying a contract for the sale of goods needs no consideration to be binding. However, UCC § 1-304 and Official Comment 2 to § 2-209 impose an overarching requirement of good faith on all contract modifications. A modification extorted without a legitimate commercial reason (e.g., merely taking advantage of the other party's vulnerability or demanding more money simply because an outsider offered a higher price) lacks good faith and is legally ineffective, even if assented to by the coerced party. Because the gallery owner manufactured the price increase solely to capture market profit from an outside bidder without any objective cost increase, the modification was in bad faith and unenforceable (Option C). Options A and B are incorrect because the modification was tainted by commercial bad faith. Option D is incorrect because the UCC expressly abolished the common law pre-existing duty rule for sales of goods; the failure turns on lack of good faith, not lack of consideration."
